// C++ client tests: run the test binary built by cpp/.build.ae. The facts
// cover the canonical tape (GET /ok -> 200 text/plain "ok-body") plus what C++
// specifically adds over the C client — RAII shutdown, move semantics,
// std::string in/out (no sv_free in user code), and exceptions instead of NULL
// returns.
//
// Uses c.tests, NOT cpp.tests, as the runner — now for ONE reason: argv.
// c.tests documents itself as a generic binary runner (bldr.program_binary_of
// — "prog may be a c.program OR an aether.program (or any future binary
// builder)") and takes run(leaf, args), so the tape path is passed in rather
// than baked into the binary with a compile-time -D define. cpp.tests passes
// no argv and no env.
//
// It used to be for two reasons. cpp.tests also ran `binary 2>&1 | tee log`,
// and a shell pipeline reports the status of its LAST command — so tee's 0
// masked a failing test binary and this leaf went green on a red suite
// (observed here: "FAILED: 1 cpp test(s)" reported as PASS). That is FIXED
// upstream — aeb's cpp/d/swift/dart/gleam/jest/moonbit/java runners now go
// through bldr._sh_tee, which captures the command's real status in an rc file
// (aeb 2734bf5). Verified after the fix: cpp.tests exits 1 with 0/1 FAIL on
// the same failing assertion. So either runner is honest now; this one is
// still the more convenient.
import bldr
import c
import c (run)
aeb(cap) {
    bldr.build() {
        prereq("gcc")
        dep("cpp/.build.ae")
        root = _get("root")
        c.tests() {
            // The tape by ABSOLUTE path: the runner runs the binary from the
            // repo root, not from cpp/, so a relative tapes/ path would
            // misresolve.
            run("cpp/.build.ae", "${root}/cpp/tapes/single_get.md")
        }
    }
}
